● Yes — there's a clean necessary condition, verified with zero exceptions across all 248,502 pairs. Write N = m+n, q = floor(N/m)
  (parity tail's stride), and r = N mod m, so N = qm + r.

  Parity tail is a sole winner only when d = gcd(q, r) > 1. Equivalently, gcd(q, N) > 1, since gcd(q, qm + r) = gcd(q, r). All 30,884
  sole wins satisfy it; none of the 142,002 eligible pairs with gcd(q, r) = 1 ever produce one.

  The mechanism: parity tail's labels are 0, q, 2q, …, (m−1)q — all multiples of q, hence all multiples of d. So the m-set sits
  entirely inside the subgroup of multiples of d in Z_N. For a set confined to that subgroup, the DFT Â(u) is periodic in u with
  period N/d — the spectrum collapses onto d identical copies of a length-N/d spectrum, and inside the subgroup the labels are again
  a near-subgroup arithmetic progression (step q/d, deficit r/d). This subgroup alignment concentrates spectral mass, which is
  exactly what shrinks ‖F‖₁ and hence FR. The balanced strategy spreads its labels maximally evenly for every (m, n) alike, so it has
  no way to exploit this arithmetic coincidence — when the coincidence occurs, parity tail's spectrum is strictly more concentrated
  and it wins outright.

  The condition is necessary but not sufficient — the win rate rises steeply with d, and prime d does better than composite:

  ┌───────────────┬───────────────┐
  │ d = gcd(q, r) │ sole-win rate │
  ├───────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ 1             │ 0% (never)    │
  ├───────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ 2             │ 23%           │
  ├───────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ 3             │ 34%           │
  ├───────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ 5             │ 52%           │
  ├───────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ 7             │ 67%           │
  ├───────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ 11            │ 76%           │
  └───────────────┴───────────────┘

  Two corollaries this explains: r = 1 never yields a sole win (gcd(q, 1) = 1 forces d = 1), and r = 0 can't either (the two
  strategies coincide there). It also explains why prime values of r were rare in the win set — d > 1 with r prime requires r to
  divide q exactly. Residual dependence on m remains (m = 3, 4, 5 never produce sole wins even when d > 1), so d > 1 is the gate, not
  the whole law.

