       77 +## Findings: when is parity tail the sole winner?                                                                  
       78 +                                                                                                                   
       79 +Empirical analysis of the MAXIMUM = 1000 run (248,502 pairs; 30,884 sole                                           
       80 +parity-tail wins). Write N = m+n, q = floor(N/m) (parity tail's stride), and                                       
       81 +r = N mod m, so N = qm + r.                                                                                        
       82 +                                                                                                                   
       83 +### Necessary condition: gcd(q, r) > 1                                                                             
       84 +                                                                                                                   
       85 +Let d = gcd(q, r), which equals gcd(q, N) since gcd(q, qm + r) = gcd(q, r).                                        
       86 +**Every sole parity-tail win has d > 1** — zero exceptions among the 30,884                                        
       87 +sole wins, and none of the 142,002 pairs with d = 1 (and r ≠ 0) is one.                                            
       88 +                                                                                                                   
       89 +Mechanism: parity tail's labels 0, q, 2q, .., (m−1)q are all multiples of q,                                       
       90 +hence of d, so the m-set lies inside the subgroup dZ_N of multiples of d.                                          
       91 +For a set confined to that subgroup, the DFT Â(u) is periodic in u with                                            
       92 +period N/d — the spectrum collapses onto d identical copies of a length-N/d                                        
       93 +spectrum — and within the subgroup the labels form again a near-subgroup                                           
       94 +arithmetic progression (step q/d, deficit r/d). This alignment concentrates                                        
       95 +spectral mass, which shrinks ‖F‖₁ and hence FR. The balanced strategy                                              
       96 +spreads its labels maximally evenly for every (m, n) alike, so it cannot                                           
       97 +exploit the arithmetic coincidence; when d > 1, parity tail's spectrum is                                          
       98 +strictly more concentrated and it can win outright.                                                                
